    {
      "id": "JbQd-IenSwI89ZDRoOG0P",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1760.1883727379522,
      "y": 3914.487352945048,
      "width": 872.539958257759,
      "height": 3784.6819181736637,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b01",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1589923310,
      "version": 3686,
      "versionNonce": 303945104,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1768743886366,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) How can we ensure that swiping is consistent and low latency?\n\n\n❌ The consistency challenge can be illustrated by:\n        → userA and userB swipe at roughly the same time\n        → Both users issue reads for the inverse swipe at the same time (but come up empty)\n        → Then both write their individual swipe relations\n\n→ This results in a Read Skew / Write Skew anomaly\n→ Isolation Means, concurrent transactions should behave as if they were executed serially.\n→ Cassandra offers serializable isolation, through Lightweight transactions on a partition key.\n→ But Cassandra LWTs are implemented using PAXOS (need multiple round trips across the\ncluster to reach concensus), which is more expensive than Isolation level/Row locking set in\nan RDBMS.\n→ RDBMS are a bad choice here because the don't support high write throughput (Usually\nsingle master)\n\n\n\n✅ GREAT SOLUTION: Sharded Cassandra with Single-Partition Transactions\n\n→ When we need consistency, we should immediately think transactions\n→ Can use cassandra's single-partition transactions transactions to atomically handle swipes\n\n        1️⃣ First create a table with compound primary key that ensures swipes between same\nusers are in same partition\n\n        CREATE TABLE swipes (\n                user_pair text,      -- partition key: smaller_id:larger_id\n                from_user uuid,      -- clustering key\n                to_user uuid,        -- clustering key\n                direction text,\n                created_at timestamp,\n                PRIMARY KEY ((user_pair), from_user, to_user)\n            );\n\n        2️⃣ When a user swipes, we create the user_pair key by sorting the IDs to ensure\nconsistency\n\n        def get_user_pair(user_a, user_b):\n                # Sort IDs so (A->B) and (B->A) are in same partition\n                sorted_ids = sorted([user_a, user_b])\n                return f\"{sorted_ids[0]}:{sorted_ids[1]}\"\n        \n        def handle_swipe(from_user, to_user, direction):\n                user_pair = get_user_pair(from_user, to_user)\n                \n                # Both operations happen atomically in same partition\n                batch = \"\"\"\n                BEGIN BATCH\n                        INSERT INTO swipes (user_pair, from_user, to_user, direction, created_at)\n                        VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?);\n                        \n                        SELECT direction FROM swipes \n                        WHERE user_pair = ? \n                        AND from_user = ? \n                        AND to_user = ?;\n                APPLY BATCH;\n                \"\"\"\n\n→ By ensuring swipes between two users are stored in same partition, we can check for\nmatches without worrying about distributed transaction complexities.\n→ Partition key design eliminates need for cross-partition operations, making solution both\nperformant and reliable\n\n\nChallenges\n→ As swipes accumulate over time, partition sizes can grow significantly, potentially\nimpacting performance (mainly read, because we will need to go to oldest ss tables)\n→ Highly active users could create hot partitions that receive a disproportionate amount of\ntraffic\n→ Need a cleanup Job to archive old swipe data, preventing partitions from growing\nunbounded while preserving important historical data (deleted/banned users, maybe matched\nrecords?)\n\n\n\n✅ GREAT SOLUTION: Redis for Atomic Operations\n\n→ Redis is a better for for handling the consistency & latency requirements:\n        * Doesn't use PAXOS, it relies on single threaded nature and ability to execute lua scripts\nto provide feature similar to ACID transactions / shard level\n        * It is also faster for reads and writes(because of redis's in-memory nature), enabling\nlower latencies\n→ Cassandra excels at durability and storing large amounts of data, it's not designed for\natomic operations we need for real-time match detection\n→ We could use a hybrid approach here:\n        → Cassandra as our durable storage layer\n        → Redis to handle atomic swipe operations\n\n→ Still need to create key that combines both uIDs in a consistent way, so swipes between\nsame users land on the same shard\n\n                Key: \"swipes:123:456\"\n                Value: {\n                    \"123_swipe\": \"right\", // or yes/no\n                    \"456_swipe\": \"left\"   // or yes/no\n                }\n\n                def get_key(user_a, user_b):\n                        # Sort IDs so (A->B) and (B->A) map to same key\n                        sorted_ids = sorted([user_a, user_b])\n                        return f\"swipes:{sorted_ids[0]}:{sorted_ids[1]}\"\n                \n                def handle_swipe(from_user, to_user, direction):\n                        key = get_key(from_user, to_user)\n                        \n                        # Use Redis hash to store both users' swipes\n                        # Each hash has two fields: user1_swipe and user2_swipe\n                        script = \"\"\"\n                        redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], ARGV[1], ARGV[2])\n                        return redis.call('HGET', KEYS[1], ARGV[3])\n                        \"\"\"\n                        \n                        # Execute atomically using Lua script\n                        other_swipe = redis.eval(\n                            script,\n                            keys=[key],\n                            args=[\n                                f\"{from_user}_swipe\",  # field to set\n                                direction,             # our swipe\n                                f\"{to_user}_swipe\"     # field to check\n                            ]\n                        )\n                        \n                        # If other user swiped right too, it's a match!\n                        if direction == 'right' and other_swipe == 'right':\n                            create_match(from_user, to_user)\n\n→ Using Redis's atomic operations via Lua scripts, we can ensure swipe recording and match\nchecking happen as a single operation\n→ Redis hash slots will enable, scaling the cluster while keeping the key on the same\nshard/node\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Additional operational complexity instroduced for the redis cluster\n        ⚫ Handle node failures\n        ⚫ Handle rebalancing\nHowever these are more manageable than trying to achieve consistency in cassandra\n→ Memory management is another consideration, we can periodically flush swipe data to\nCassandra and maintain only recent swipes in Redis\n→ If we lose Redis data due to a node failure, we're only losing the ability to detect matches\nfor very recent swipes - users can always swipe again, & we're not losing historical record in\nCassandra\n→ Use a hybrid approach, system remains highly available and scalable while meeting our\ncore requirement of consistent, immediate match detection",
      "fontSize": 20.59690839822402,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) How can we ensure that swiping is consistent and low latency?\n\n\n❌ The consistency challenge can be illustrated by:\n        → userA and userB swipe at roughly the same time\n        → Both users issue reads for the inverse swipe at the same time (but come up empty)\n        → Then both write their individual swipe relations\n\n→ This results in a Read Skew / Write Skew anomaly\n→ Isolation Means, concurrent transactions should behave as if they were executed serially.\n→ Cassandra offers serializable isolation, through Lightweight transactions on a partition key.\n→ But Cassandra LWTs are implemented using PAXOS (need multiple round trips across the cluster to reach concensus), which is more expensive than Isolation level/Row locking set in an RDBMS.\n→ RDBMS are a bad choice here because the don't support high write throughput (Usually single master)\n\n\n\n✅ GREAT SOLUTION: Sharded Cassandra with Single-Partition Transactions\n\n→ When we need consistency, we should immediately think transactions\n→ Can use cassandra's single-partition transactions transactions to atomically handle swipes\n\n        1️⃣ First create a table with compound primary key that ensures swipes between same users are in same partition\n\n        CREATE TABLE swipes (\n                user_pair text,      -- partition key: smaller_id:larger_id\n                from_user uuid,      -- clustering key\n                to_user uuid,        -- clustering key\n                direction text,\n                created_at timestamp,\n                PRIMARY KEY ((user_pair), from_user, to_user)\n            );\n\n        2️⃣ When a user swipes, we create the user_pair key by sorting the IDs to ensure consistency\n\n        def get_user_pair(user_a, user_b):\n                # Sort IDs so (A->B) and (B->A) are in same partition\n                sorted_ids = sorted([user_a, user_b])\n                return f\"{sorted_ids[0]}:{sorted_ids[1]}\"\n        \n        def handle_swipe(from_user, to_user, direction):\n                user_pair = get_user_pair(from_user, to_user)\n                \n                # Both operations happen atomically in same partition\n                batch = \"\"\"\n                BEGIN BATCH\n                        INSERT INTO swipes (user_pair, from_user, to_user, direction, created_at)\n                        VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?);\n                        \n                        SELECT direction FROM swipes \n                        WHERE user_pair = ? \n                        AND from_user = ? \n                        AND to_user = ?;\n                APPLY BATCH;\n                \"\"\"\n\n→ By ensuring swipes between two users are stored in same partition, we can check for matches without worrying about distributed transaction complexities.\n→ Partition key design eliminates need for cross-partition operations, making solution both performant and reliable\n\n\nChallenges\n→ As swipes accumulate over time, partition sizes can grow significantly, potentially impacting performance (mainly read, because we will need to go to oldest ss tables)\n→ Highly active users could create hot partitions that receive a disproportionate amount of traffic\n→ Need a cleanup Job to archive old swipe data, preventing partitions from growing unbounded while preserving important historical data (deleted/banned users, maybe matched records?)\n\n\n\n✅ GREAT SOLUTION: Redis for Atomic Operations\n\n→ Redis is a better for for handling the consistency & latency requirements:\n        * Doesn't use PAXOS, it relies on single threaded nature and ability to execute lua scripts to provide feature similar to ACID transactions / shard level\n        * It is also faster for reads and writes(because of redis's in-memory nature), enabling lower latencies\n→ Cassandra excels at durability and storing large amounts of data, it's not designed for atomic operations we need for real-time match detection\n→ We could use a hybrid approach here:\n        → Cassandra as our durable storage layer\n        → Redis to handle atomic swipe operations\n\n→ Still need to create key that combines both uIDs in a consistent way, so swipes between same users land on the same shard\n\n                Key: \"swipes:123:456\"\n                Value: {\n                    \"123_swipe\": \"right\", // or yes/no\n                    \"456_swipe\": \"left\"   // or yes/no\n                }\n\n                def get_key(user_a, user_b):\n                        # Sort IDs so (A->B) and (B->A) map to same key\n                        sorted_ids = sorted([user_a, user_b])\n                        return f\"swipes:{sorted_ids[0]}:{sorted_ids[1]}\"\n                \n                def handle_swipe(from_user, to_user, direction):\n                        key = get_key(from_user, to_user)\n                        \n                        # Use Redis hash to store both users' swipes\n                        # Each hash has two fields: user1_swipe and user2_swipe\n                        script = \"\"\"\n                        redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], ARGV[1], ARGV[2])\n                        return redis.call('HGET', KEYS[1], ARGV[3])\n                        \"\"\"\n                        \n                        # Execute atomically using Lua script\n                        other_swipe = redis.eval(\n                            script,\n                            keys=[key],\n                            args=[\n                                f\"{from_user}_swipe\",  # field to set\n                                direction,             # our swipe\n                                f\"{to_user}_swipe\"     # field to check\n                            ]\n                        )\n                        \n                        # If other user swiped right too, it's a match!\n                        if direction == 'right' and other_swipe == 'right':\n                            create_match(from_user, to_user)\n\n→ Using Redis's atomic operations via Lua scripts, we can ensure swipe recording and match checking happen as a single operation\n→ Redis hash slots will enable, scaling the cluster while keeping the key on the same shard/node\n\n\nChallenges\n→ Additional operational complexity instroduced for the redis cluster\n        ⚫ Handle node failures\n        ⚫ Handle rebalancing\nHowever these are more manageable than trying to achieve consistency in cassandra\n→ Memory management is another consideration, we can periodically flush swipe data to Cassandra and maintain only recent swipes in Redis\n→ If we lose Redis data due to a node failure, we're only losing the ability to detect matches for very recent swipes - users can always swipe again, & we're not losing historical record in Cassandra\n→ Use a hybrid approach, system remains highly available and scalable while meeting our core requirement of consistent, immediate match detection",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pIPigoQ79kMTSulNiV_td",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2715.3825961149314,
