      "seed": 1236273685,
      "version": 1359,
      "versionNonce": 2000575376,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1768741661314,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Key intuition here is to use a single key to model this as a bidirectional\nrelation:\n    → Constructible & accessible from within either users swipe requests\n    → Minimally constructed, using both uids in a consistent way\n    → Records a YES | NO, for both directions (a→b & b→a)\n\ndef get_user_pair(user_a, user_b):\n        # Sort IDs so (A->B) and (B->A) are in same partition\n        sorted_ids = sorted([user_a, user_b])\n        return f\"{sorted_ids[0]}:{sorted_ids[1]}\"\n\n\n→ UserA as partition Key\n→ UserB as clustering Key\n→ In this scenario, we will use redis lua script transaction to ensure\nconsistent write and read\n→ Advantage: keys can be distributed across shards/partitions\nrandomly, avoiding hot shards for users who swipe a lot (as is the case\nwith cassandra)\n→ Cassandra can still be used for persistance, and high write ingestion\n\n\nAlternate schema that works (fuctionally),\n→ UserA:UserB as partition Key\n→ UserA as clustering Key\n→ UserB also as clustering Key\n\nIn this case we will use cassandra's lightweight transactions that are\nconsistent within a partition\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "Z36CqDJ2uxjkTHC4TXDNy",
      "originalText": "Key intuition here is to use a single key to model this as a bidirectional relation:\n    → Constructible & accessible from within either users swipe requests\n    → Minimally constructed, using both uids in a consistent way\n    → Records a YES | NO, for both directions (a→b & b→a)\n\ndef get_user_pair(user_a, user_b):\n        # Sort IDs so (A->B) and (B->A) are in same partition\n        sorted_ids = sorted([user_a, user_b])\n        return f\"{sorted_ids[0]}:{sorted_ids[1]}\"\n\n\n→ UserA as partition Key\n→ UserB as clustering Key\n→ In this scenario, we will use redis lua script transaction to ensure consistent write and read\n→ Advantage: keys can be distributed across shards/partitions randomly, avoiding hot shards for users who swipe a lot (as is the case with cassandra)\n→ Cassandra can still be used for persistance, and high write ingestion\n\n\nAlternate schema that works (fuctionally),\n→ UserA:UserB as partition Key\n→ UserA as clustering Key\n→ UserB also as clustering Key\n\nIn this case we will use cassandra's lightweight transactions that are consistent within a partition\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "7TlMKi1jbUnrs5ui1Calb",
      "type": "ellipse",
      "x": 4291.5543968761385,
      "y": 1512.7850526602667,
      "width": 226.0814322710901,
      "height": 226.0814322710903,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
