    {
      "id": "VYs0O-k-0FbqbyCO2E_JP",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 7458.896443155793,
      "y": 679.0122748581059,
      "width": 626.9793701171875,
      "height": 2725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2L",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 484970216,
      "version": 130,
      "versionNonce": 1586953733,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1768923805422,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1️⃣ Public S3 Object URL (object is public)\n\nIf the object is publicly readable, the URL is deterministic.\n\nURL format\nhttps://<bucket-name>.s3.<region>.amazonaws.com/<key>\n\nExample\nhttps://my-bucket.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/file.txt\n\nHow to make it public (not always recommended)\n\nBucket policy or object ACL allows s3:GetObject\n\n{\n  \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n  \"Principal\": \"*\",\n  \"Action\": \"s3:GetObject\",\n  \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/uploads/*\"\n}\n\n\n⚠️ Use with caution for sensitive files.\n\n2️⃣ CloudFront URL (recommended for production)\n\nIf your bucket is behind CloudFront, this is the URL you should return.\n\nURL format\nhttps://<distribution-domain>/<key>\n\nExample\nhttps://d3abc123.cloudfront.net/uploads/file.txt\n\n\n✔️ Faster\n✔️ Can keep S3 private\n✔️ Supports signed URLs/cookies\n\n3️⃣ Presigned GET URL (private objects) ✅\n\nIf the object is private, generate a temporary URL.\n\nNode.js\nimport { GetObjectCommand } from \"@aws-sdk/client-s3\";\nimport { getSignedUrl } from \"@aws-sdk/s3-request-presigner\";\n\nconst command = new GetObjectCommand({\n  Bucket: \"my-bucket\",\n  Key: \"uploads/file.txt\"\n});\n\nconst url = await getSignedUrl(s3, command, { expiresIn: 3600 }); // 1\nhour\n\nPython\nurl = s3.generate_presigned_url(\n    \"get_object\",\n    Params={\"Bucket\": \"my-bucket\", \"Key\": \"uploads/file.txt\"},\n    ExpiresIn=3600\n)\n\n\n✔️ Secure\n✔️ Time-limited\n✔️ No public access required\n\n4️⃣ Getting the URL at upload time\nUsing AWS SDKs\n\nMost SDKs do not return a URL automatically.\nYou construct it yourself from:\n\nBucket\n\nKey\n\nRegion\n\nExample (Node.js)\nconst url = `https://${bucket}.s3.${region}.amazonaws.com/${key}`;\n\n5️⃣ Common mistake ❌\n\n“S3 gave me a random URL”\n\nS3 never generates random URLs.\nThe URL is always derived from the object key.\n\nIf you know:\n\nBucket: my-bucket\nKey: uploads/image.png\n\n\nThen the object path is always:\n\n/uploads/image.png\n\nWhich URL should you return?\nScenario        URL to return\nPublic file        S3 URL\nPrivate file        Presigned GET URL\nWeb / mobile app        CloudFront URL\nSensitive data        Presigned URL only\nBest practice ⭐\n\nStore only the object key (uploads/file.txt) in your DB.\nGenerate URLs on demand.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "9ZVfE3MpUvfSKDnbl3A0f",
      "originalText": "1️⃣ Public S3 Object URL (object is public)\n\nIf the object is publicly readable, the URL is deterministic.\n\nURL format\nhttps://<bucket-name>.s3.<region>.amazonaws.com/<key>\n\nExample\nhttps://my-bucket.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/file.txt\n\nHow to make it public (not always recommended)\n\nBucket policy or object ACL allows s3:GetObject\n\n{\n  \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n  \"Principal\": \"*\",\n  \"Action\": \"s3:GetObject\",\n  \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/uploads/*\"\n}\n\n\n⚠️ Use with caution for sensitive files.\n\n2️⃣ CloudFront URL (recommended for production)\n\nIf your bucket is behind CloudFront, this is the URL you should return.\n\nURL format\nhttps://<distribution-domain>/<key>\n\nExample\nhttps://d3abc123.cloudfront.net/uploads/file.txt\n\n\n✔️ Faster\n✔️ Can keep S3 private\n✔️ Supports signed URLs/cookies\n\n3️⃣ Presigned GET URL (private objects) ✅\n\nIf the object is private, generate a temporary URL.\n\nNode.js\nimport { GetObjectCommand } from \"@aws-sdk/client-s3\";\nimport { getSignedUrl } from \"@aws-sdk/s3-request-presigner\";\n\nconst command = new GetObjectCommand({\n  Bucket: \"my-bucket\",\n  Key: \"uploads/file.txt\"\n});\n\nconst url = await getSignedUrl(s3, command, { expiresIn: 3600 }); // 1 hour\n\nPython\nurl = s3.generate_presigned_url(\n    \"get_object\",\n    Params={\"Bucket\": \"my-bucket\", \"Key\": \"uploads/file.txt\"},\n    ExpiresIn=3600\n)\n\n\n✔️ Secure\n✔️ Time-limited\n✔️ No public access required\n\n4️⃣ Getting the URL at upload time\nUsing AWS SDKs\n\nMost SDKs do not return a URL automatically.\nYou construct it yourself from:\n\nBucket\n\nKey\n\nRegion\n\nExample (Node.js)\nconst url = `https://${bucket}.s3.${region}.amazonaws.com/${key}`;\n\n5️⃣ Common mistake ❌\n\n“S3 gave me a random URL”\n\nS3 never generates random URLs.\nThe URL is always derived from the object key.\n\nIf you know:\n\nBucket: my-bucket\nKey: uploads/image.png\n\n\nThen the object path is always:\n\n/uploads/image.png\n\nWhich URL should you return?\nScenario        URL to return\nPublic file        S3 URL\nPrivate file        Presigned GET URL\nWeb / mobile app        CloudFront URL\nSensitive data        Presigned URL only\nBest practice ⭐\n\nStore only the object key (uploads/file.txt) in your DB.\nGenerate URLs on demand.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
